UID:a4-residency-chengdu-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260924T143909Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261231
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20270101
SUMMARY:Deadline: A4 Residency Art Center: International Residency Fellowsh
 ip 2028 (Chengdu)
DESCRIPTION:The A4 Residency Art Center in Chengdu\, the residency platform
  of the A4 Art Museum in Luxelakes Eco-City\, opens its full-year 2028 rec
 ruitment. The interdisciplinary\, open and collaborative platform fosters 
 creative exchange among artists\, scientists\, writers and other professio
 nals\, with residents initiating participatory local activities that conne
 ct them with the city and community\; A4 also organises group activities\,
  sharing sessions\, media promotion and exhibition opportunities. CYCLES: 
 Spring 2028 (April to June) and Autumn 2028 (July to October)\, each appro
 ximately eight weeks. WHAT YOU GET (International Residency Fellowship\, f
 ully funded): private studio and accommodation\, round-trip economy airfar
 e or second-class train within a set budget\, a production grant of RMB 10
 \,000 per person or team for materials and production (excluding living ex
 penses)\, community engagement and cultural experiences\, media promotion 
 and networking. ELIGIBILITY: multidisciplinary creators worldwide\, includ
 ing visual and performing artists\, musicians\, filmmakers\, architects\, 
 curators\, designers and creatives with interdisciplinary backgrounds brid
 ging art\, business and culture. APPLICATION: free\; submit a photo\, port
 folio\, CV and project proposal in English and Chinese to a4residency@a4am
 .cn\, with the subject line specifying the programme tier and preferred cy
 cle. DEADLINE: 31 December 2026 for the 2028 full-year recruitment. A para
 llel self-funded light track exists\; this listing covers the funded fello
 wship only.\n\nOrganisation: A4 Residency Art Center (A4 Art Museum)\nLoca
 tion: Luxelakes Eco-City\, Tianfu New Area\, Chengdu\, China. Open worldwi
 de.\nAward: Fully funded fellowship: private studio and accommodation\, ro
 und-trip economy airfare or second-class train ticket within a set budget\
 , and a production grant of RMB 10\,000 per person/team for materials and 
 production (excluding living expenses). No application fee.\nDuration: App
 roximately 8 weeks\, in the Spring 2028 (April to June) or Autumn 2028 (Ju
 ly to October) cycle.\n\nMore: https://www.a4artmuseum.com/en/a4-residency
 artcenter/residing/latest-recruitment/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantde
 sk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.a4artmuseum.com/en/a4-residencyartcenter/residing/latest-re
 cruitment/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Luxelakes Eco-City\, Tianfu New Area\, Chengdu\, China. Open world
 wide.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:camargo-fellowship-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260924T143909Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261001
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261002
SUMMARY:Deadline: Camargo Fellowship 2027-2028 for Artists\, Scholars and T
 hinkers (Cassis\, France)
DESCRIPTION:The Camargo Foundation invites artists\, scholars and thinkers 
 to its flagship Core Fellowship: a ten-week self-directed residency on its
  clifftop campus overlooking the Mediterranean in Cassis\, France\, dedica
 ted to research\, experimentation and interdisciplinary exchange. Curators
  apply under the Thinkers category\, which covers accomplished professiona
 ls and practitioners in cultural and creative fields who are professionall
 y engaged in critical thought\, including curators\, critics and journalis
 ts\; projects should engage theoretical\, artistic or societal questions. 
 WHAT YOU GET: a stipend of EUR 350 per week (EUR 3\,500 total)\, basic tra
 nsportation funding\, and a furnished apartment with an equipped kitchen o
 n campus. Fourteen fellowships are awarded per year: seven for artists and
  seven for scholars and thinkers. RESIDENCY PERIODS: Fall 2027 (8 Septembe
 r to 17 November 2027) or Spring 2028 (23 February to 4 May 2028). ELIGIBI
 LITY: artists in visual arts\, performance\, writing\, film\, music or mul
 tidisciplinary work with at least 5 years of professional practice\; schol
 ars holding or pursuing a PhD focused on French and Francophone cultures o
 r cross-cultural Mediterranean studies\; and thinkers as described above. 
 APPLICATION: via Submittable with a 100-word proposal summary\, a 1\,200-w
 ord proposal narrative\, a 300-word rationale for Camargo\, a 300-word car
 eer-stage rationale\, a CV (max 3 pages)\, work samples and two references
 . NOTE: the foundation has announced that this is the final Fellowship sel
 ected through a public open call until further notice\; a new selection pr
 ocess begins in July 2027\, making this the last chance to apply directly.
 \n\nOrganisation: Camargo Foundation\nLocation: Camargo Foundation campus\
 , Cassis\, France. Open worldwide.\nAward: EUR 350 per week (EUR 3\,500 to
 tal for the ten-week residency)\, plus basic travel funding and a furnishe
 d apartment with equipped kitchen on the Camargo campus. 14 fellowships pe
 r year: 7 for artists and 7 for scholars and thinkers. No application fee.
 \nDuration: Ten weeks\, either 8 September to 17 November 2027 or 23 Febru
 ary to 4 May 2028.\n\nMore: https://camargofoundation.org/en/camargo-fello
 wship-2026-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://camargofoundation.org/en/camargo-fellowship-2026-2027?utm_sourc
 e=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Camargo Foundation campus\, Cassis\, France. Open worldwide.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:guggenheim-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260924T143909Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260916
SUMMARY:Deadline: Guggenheim Fellowship 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Guggenheim Fellowship is awarded to mid-career individuals 
 who have already demonstrated exceptional capacity for productive scholars
 hip or exceptional creative ability in the arts\, giving them six to twelv
 e months of funded time to work with as much creative freedom as possible.
  Around 175 to 200 fellows are chosen each year from a field of several th
 ousand applicants (a success rate of roughly five percent)\, across more t
 han 50 fields spanning the creative arts\, the humanities\, the natural sc
 iences and the social sciences\, including architecture\, film\, music\, p
 hotography\, fine arts\, computer science and interdisciplinary work. ELIG
 IBILITY: citizens and permanent residents of the United States and Canada\
 ; applicants must be mid-career with a significant record of published or 
 exhibited work\, and current students and previous Guggenheim Fellows are 
 not eligible. APPLICATION: through the Foundation's own portal\, which ope
 ns in late summer 2026\, so check the site if it is not yet live. The appl
 ication deadline is 15 September 2026 with no late submissions accepted\; 
 work samples are due 29 September 2026 and reference letters 13 October 20
 26. Results are announced in late April 2027. No fee.\n\nOrganisation: Joh
 n Simon Guggenheim Memorial Foundation\nLocation: Anywhere\; the fellowshi
 p funds the fellow's own work rather than residence at a host institution.
  Open to citizens and permanent residents of the United States and Canada.
 \nAward: Variable cash grant sized to the applicant's proposed budget and 
 needs\; the Foundation does not publish a fixed figure or range\, and rece
 nt awards have typically run into the tens of thousands of US dollars. Rou
 ghly 175 to 200 fellowships are awarded each year across more than 50 fiel
 ds. The Foundation charges no application fee and has no affiliated applic
 ation consultants.\nDuration: Six to twelve months of supported work\n\nMo
 re: https://www.gf.org/program/how-to-apply/?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.gf.org/program/how-to-apply/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Anywhere\; the fellowship funds the fellow's own work rather than 
 residence at a host institution. Open to citizens and permanent residents 
 of the United States and Canada.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:american-academy-berlin-prize-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260924T143909Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260925
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260926
SUMMARY:Deadline: American Academy in Berlin: Berlin Prize Fellowship 2027-
 28
DESCRIPTION:The American Academy in Berlin is an independent\, nonprofit in
 stitute for advanced study in the arts\, humanities\, social sciences\, la
 w\, journalism and public policy\, whose primary mission is to strengthen 
 the cultural and intellectual ties between the United States and Germany. 
 Each year\, after a rigorous selection process\, the Academy awards roughl
 y two dozen semester-long Berlin Prize fellowships to individuals who demo
 nstrate excellence in their fields. DISCIPLINES: applications are welcome 
 across the humanities\, social sciences\, law\, journalism and various are
 as of public policy. The Academy generally encourages projects addressing 
 pressing issues of the day\, specifically naming democracy\, security\, te
 chnology and society (especially AI)\, energy including climate\, and glob
 al order. Dedicated fellowships also support projects in Jewish studies\, 
 German studies\, and public health and biotechnology. Project proposals ne
 ed not focus on Germany\, but candidates should explain how a residency in
  Berlin would enhance their work. NOT ACCEPTED: proposals in mathematics o
 r the natural sciences. NOTE FOR ARTISTS: visual artists and composers are
  invited through separate\, nomination-based competitions\, so there is no
  open application route for them here. ELIGIBILITY: fellowships are restri
 cted to citizens and permanent residents of the United States. Both emergi
 ng and established figures are eligible. PhD holders qualify as emerging s
 cholars with a strong record of peer-reviewed work beyond the dissertation
 \; candidates in non-academic fields such as journalism\, filmmaking and p
 olicy need a significant record of publication or production\; fiction and
  nonfiction writers must have published at least one book with a reputable
  press. WHAT FELLOWS DO: fellows present their work to audiences in and ar
 ound Berlin through lectures\, readings\, public discussions\, performance
 s and film screenings\, and should be willing to engage with relevant Germ
 an institutions\, organizations and the media\; these activities are integ
 ral to the Academy's mission of fostering transatlantic dialogue. APPLICAT
 ION MATERIALS: a project proposal of 1\,500 to 1\,750 words plus a one-pag
 e bibliography\; a curriculum vitae of up to 10 pages\; two writing sample
 s totalling no more than 60 pages\; and three letters of recommendation\, 
 which are due 2 October 2026. DEADLINE: Friday 25 September 2026 at 12:00 
 noon Eastern Time (18:00 Central European Time). TIMELINE: all candidates 
 are notified of the competition outcome by email in late March 2027\; the 
 2027-28 Berlin Prize recipients are announced in May 2027.\n\nOrganisation
 : American Academy in Berlin\nLocation: Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arn
 hold Center. RESTRICTED: fellowships are open only to citizens and permane
 nt residents of the United States.\nAward: Monthly stipend of USD 5\,000\,
  plus roundtrip airfare\, accommodation at or near the Hans Arnhold Center
 \, partial board\, in-house library services and professional networking o
 pportunities. Roughly two dozen fellowships awarded each year. No applicat
 ion fee.\nDuration: One full academic semester (about 4 months). Fall runs
  mid-August to mid-December\; spring runs late January to late May.\n\nMor
 e: https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arnhold Center. RESTRICTED: fellows
 hips are open only to citizens and permanent residents of the United State
 s.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:harvard-radcliffe-institute-fellowship-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.gith
 ub.io
DTSTAMP:20260924T143909Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260910
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260911
SUMMARY:Deadline: Harvard Radcliffe Institute: Fellowship Program 2027-2028
DESCRIPTION:The Harvard Radcliffe Institute Fellowship offers scientists\, 
 writers\, scholars\, public intellectuals\, and artists a year to pursue a
 mbitious projects in a vibrant interdisciplinary setting amid the resource
 s of Harvard University. The Institute welcomes innovative work that confr
 onts pressing social\, scientific\, and policy issues and seeks to engage 
 audiences beyond academia. Reflecting Radcliffe's history\, it welcomes (b
 ut does not limit eligibility to) proposals focused on women\, gender\, an
 d society\, or that draw on the Schlesinger Library's collections. It also
  invites proposals relevant to the Institute's 2024-2029 focus area\, acad
 emic freedom and connecting across difference (intellectual virtues\, free
  and open inquiry\, diversity of thought\, political polarization\, peace 
 and conflict\, inequality\, religious pluralism\, and related higher-educa
 tion policy issues)\, including work that constructively challenges discip
 linary orthodoxies or advances transformative perspectives. SUPPORT: USD 7
 8\,000 stipend plus USD 5\,000 for project expenses\, with relocation\, ho
 using\, and childcare funds available\; fellows must reside in the Cambrid
 ge/Boston area from September through May. ELIGIBILITY: open to individual
 s across career stages\; tenure is not required and applicants need not be
  academics. This is NOT a postdoctoral fellowship\; those currently enroll
 ed in a degree program are ineligible\, as are former Harvard Radcliffe fe
 llows (1999-present). Applicants must meet discipline-specific criteria. A
 PPLY: register on the online portal and select an area (Humanities and Soc
 ial Sciences\; Creative Arts\; Nonfiction and Journalism\; or Science\, En
 gineering\, and Mathematics). Materials: application form\, CV\, 1\,400-wo
 rd project proposal (with bibliography when appropriate)\, a writing or wo
 rk sample\, and three references. DEADLINES: humanities\, social sciences\
 , creative arts\, and nonfiction and journalism by 10 September 2026\, 5pm
  ET\; science\, engineering\, and mathematics by 1 October 2026\, 5pm ET.\
 n\nOrganisation: Harvard Radcliffe Institute\nLocation: Cambridge\, Massac
 husetts\, USA\; fellows must relocate to and reside in the Cambridge/Bosto
 n area for the full fellowship (September through May).\nAward: USD 78\,00
 0 stipend plus an additional USD 5\,000 for project expenses. Relocation\,
  housing\, and childcare funds may also be provided. No application fee.\n
 Duration: One academic year\, September 2027 through May 2028 (residential
 )\n\nMore: https://www.radcliffe.harvard.edu/radcliffe-fellowship/applicat
 ion-information?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.radcliffe.harvard.edu/radcliffe-fellowship/application-info
 rmation?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Cambridge\, Massachusetts\, USA\; fellows must relocate to and res
 ide in the Cambridge/Boston area for the full fellowship (September throug
 h May).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:canon-foundation-europe-research-fellowships-2026@artificialnouveau.git
 hub.io
DTSTAMP:20260924T143909Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260916
SUMMARY:Deadline: Canon Foundation in Europe Research Fellowships 2026 (Eur
 ope–Japan Exchange)
DESCRIPTION:Canon Foundation in Europe Research Fellowships support bilater
 al research exchange between Europe and Japan\, awarding up to 15 fellowsh
 ips annually. AMOUNT: up to EUR 30\,000 per year (pro-rated for shorter st
 ays)\, flexible — covers living costs\, travel\, insurance\, research ex
 penses\, and books\; fellows determine their own allocation. ELIGIBILITY: 
 European nationals (including UK\, Israel\, Switzerland\, Turkey\, Balkan 
 and Baltic countries) conducting research in Japan\; or Japanese nationals
  conducting research in Europe. Applicants must have completed at least a 
 Master's or PhD degree within the last 10 years (older qualifications may 
 apply with supporting documentation). No restrictions on research field. P
 riority given to first-time applicants to Europe/Japan. Fellows arrange th
 eir own host institution and advisor. Up to 15 fellowships awarded annuall
 y. Deadline: 15 September 2026\, 23:59 CET (for fellowships starting Janua
 ry-December 2027). Apply at https://portal.canonfoundation.org/. Contact: 
 canon.foundation@canon-europe.com.\n\nOrganisation: Canon Foundation in Eu
 rope\nLocation: Europe or Japan (bilateral exchange\; fellows arrange own 
 host institution)\nAward: Up to EUR 30\,000 per year (pro-rated for shorte
 r stays). No application fee.\nDuration: Minimum 3 months\, maximum 1 year
 . Fellowships for 2027 start.\n\nMore: https://portal.canonfoundation.org/
 ?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://portal.canonfoundation.org/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantde
 sk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Europe or Japan (bilateral exchange\; fellows arrange own host ins
 titution)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:kometa-fellows-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260924T143909Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260731
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260801
SUMMARY:Deadline: Kometa Fellows 2026: Field Journalism\, Photography\, and
  Writing
DESCRIPTION:Kometa\, a French literary and journalism magazine\, awards fel
 lowships to journalists\, writers\, and photographers documenting reality 
 in contexts of war\, censorship\, or severe social and political tensions.
  Projects must be rooted in direct field experience and focused on contemp
 orary rather than historical events. Projects already under a contractual 
 agreement are ineligible. AMOUNT: €10\,000 per Fellow\, six Fellows sele
 cted. WHAT FELLOWS RECEIVE: one year of funding\; publication of the compl
 eted project (partial or full) in Kometa\; opportunity to contribute to th
 e monthly newsletter\; mentorship through editorial collaboration. 2026 FO
 CUS AREAS: disinformation and narrative wars\; political and social change
 s in the Gulf. DEADLINE: 31 July 2026. APPLY via the Tally form (https://t
 ally.so/r/J9XxMK)\, in French or English (about 30 to 45 minutes). Contact
 : fellows@kometarevue.com.\n\nOrganisation: Kometa (French literary and jo
 urnalism magazine)\nLocation: Remote / field-based (international)\nAward:
  €10\,000 per Fellow\, six Fellows selected. No application fee.\nDurati
 on: One year of funding for field work and research\n\nMore: https://komet
 arevue.com/page/fellows?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=r
 eferral
URL:https://kometarevue.com/page/fellows?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantd
 esk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote / field-based (international)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:columbia-ideas-imagination-paris-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260924T143909Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: Columbia Institute for Ideas and Imagination: Paris Fello
 wship 2027-28
DESCRIPTION:Columbia University's Institute for Ideas and Imagination bring
 s a cohort of scholars and artists to Reid Hall in Paris for an academic y
 ear (September 2027 to May 2028)\, with a USD 75\,000 stipend\, housing\, 
 and research support. The creative-arts category explicitly includes visua
 l and sound arts and film alongside writing and composition. ELIGIBILITY: 
 any nationality\, but artists must not be permanently residentent in the U
 nited States (Netherlands-based artists qualify). Deadline 30 September 20
 26.\n\nOrganisation: Columbia University Institute for Ideas and Imaginati
 on\nLocation: Paris\, France (Reid Hall). Any nationality\; artists must N
 OT be permanently resident in the United States.\nAward: USD 75\,000 stipe
 nd\, plus housing at Reid Hall in Paris and office and research support\, 
 September 2027 to May 2028. No application fee.\nDuration: Fellowship year
  September 2027 to May 2028 at Reid Hall\, Paris.\n\nMore: https://ideasim
 agination.columbia.edu/apply/applying-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source=artific
 ialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://ideasimagination.columbia.edu/apply/applying-for-a-fellowship/?
 utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Paris\, France (Reid Hall). Any nationality\; artists must NOT be 
 permanently resident in the United States.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:helpmann-fellowships-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260924T143909Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: Helpmann Fellowships 2026
DESCRIPTION:Three Helpmann Fellowships of up to AUD 7\,500 support ambitiou
 s\, self-directed projects that advance the artistic and professional deve
 lopment of emerging creatives. Fellowships are open across all eligible di
 sciplines\, to individuals and to collectives\, and funding can go to larg
 er-scale creative projects\, tailored professional development\, or a comb
 ination of both. Previous fellowships have supported the creation of new w
 ork\, exhibitions and performances\, residencies and internships\, touring
  and recording\, specialised training\, and long-term professional develop
 ment\; each fellowship is shaped by the recipient rather than a fixed prog
 ramme. ELIGIBILITY IS NARROW AND GEOGRAPHIC: applicants must be current re
 sidents of South Australia and either final-year students or graduates wit
 hin five years of a supported degree at Adelaide University\, the Universi
 ty of Adelaide\, Flinders University\, or the University of South Australi
 a. Honours students and graduates (up to five years after the final year o
 f study) are eligible where honours was undertaken directly following the 
 undergraduate degree. Masters and PhD candidates and graduates are not eli
 gible unless they qualify through their undergraduate or honours study. Fi
 nal-year students may apply provided the funded activity takes place after
  studies are completed and within the activity timeframe. Applications are
  assessed on artistic merit\, alignment with career development\, feasibil
 ity\, commitment to learning\, and level of ambition. The online applicati
 on requires a CV (max two pages)\, an official academic transcript\, a bud
 get on the Helpmann template\, a timeline\, project confirmations\, letter
 s of support from an academic and an industry referee\, and four to six im
 ages or one to two audio-visual work samples. An online information sessio
 n runs 4 August 2026 at 6pm ACST\, and the Program Manager\, Toni Wills\, 
 takes online or in-person application chats (info@helpmannacademy.com.au).
  Applications opened 15 July 2026 and close 31 August 2026 at 11:59pm ACST
 \, for activity occurring 1 January to 31 December 2027. Free to apply.\n\
 nOrganisation: Helpmann Academy\nLocation: South Australia (applicant must
  be a current South Australian resident)\; funded activity may take place 
 anywhere.\nAward: Up to AUD 7\,500 per fellowship\, three fellowships avai
 lable. Supported by the James & Diana Ramsay Foundation. No application fe
 e.\nDuration: Funded activity must occur between 1 January 2027 and 31 Dec
 ember 2027\n\nMore: https://helpmannacademy.com.au/helpmann-fellowships/?u
 tm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://helpmannacademy.com.au/helpmann-fellowships/?utm_source=artific
 ialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:South Australia (applicant must be a current South Australian resi
 dent)\; funded activity may take place anywhere.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ual-storytelling-research-fellows-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260924T143909Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260911
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260912
SUMMARY:Deadline: UAL AKO Storytelling Institute: Storytelling Research Fel
 lows 2026 (six posts)
DESCRIPTION:The UAL AKO Storytelling Institute\, with the Power of Pop Fund
 \, is recruiting six Storytelling Research Fellows to run independent rese
 arch projects on how storytelling in popular culture shapes attitudes\, be
 haviour and public discourse. Fellows work across media including televisi
 on\, film\, gaming\, podcasts and digital culture\, are supported by the I
 nstitute's Head of Research and a peer cohort\, and produce actionable fin
 dings for creative practitioners\, funders and social-impact organisations
 \, presented at a closing public symposium. ELIGIBILITY: experienced resea
 rchers with a PhD or equivalent research expertise gained academically\, i
 ndependently or through professional practice\, with a track record of del
 ivering high-quality research and working with external partners. Practice
 -based and non-academic researchers are explicitly in scope. APPLICATION: 
 CV\, record of publications\, a research proposal of 1\,000 to 1\,200 word
 s\, and a supporting statement including at least one example of a researc
 h project you designed and delivered. Applications via the UAL jobs portal
 \; closes 11 September 2026.\n\nOrganisation: University of the Arts Londo
 n (UAL AKO Storytelling Institute)\, with the Power of Pop Fund\nLocation:
  High Holborn\, London\, United Kingdom (hybrid)\nAward: GBP 48\,563 per a
 nnum pro rata (around GBP 19\,425 for the part-time fellowship hours)\, pl
 us UAL benefits. No application fee.\nDuration: Nine-month fellowship\, pa
 rt-time\, culminating in a public symposium\n\nMore: https://jobs.arts.ac.
 uk/job/12629-storytelling-research-fellows/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://jobs.arts.ac.uk/job/12629-storytelling-research-fellows/?utm_so
 urce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:High Holborn\, London\, United Kingdom (hybrid)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:agci-climate-environment-data-stewardship-fellow-2026@artificialnouveau
 .github.io
DTSTAMP:20260924T143909Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260819
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260820
SUMMARY:Deadline: Aspen Global Change Institute: Climate and Environment Da
 ta Stewardship Fellow
DESCRIPTION:AGCI is hiring a Climate and Environment Data Stewardship Fello
 w to coordinate and contribute to cross-organizational climate and environ
 mental data stewardship efforts\, supporting synergies and collective lear
 ning across established initiatives and networks focused on the collection
  and management of data used to inform climate action\, in ways that promo
 te long-term data quality\, accessibility\, interoperability and security.
  PLACEMENT AND MENTORSHIP: the fellow sits within AGCI's Engaged Research 
 Team\, reporting to AGCI's Research Director\, with day-to-day mentorship 
 from leaders at core partner organisations in the data stewardship space\,
  including the Data Foundation (Sonia Wang) and Public Environmental Data 
 Partners' co-founding member the Open Environmental Data Project (Brittany
  Janis)\, plus guidance from other leaders in the field. RESPONSIBILITIES:
  coordination of the coordinators\, using a network-of-networks approach t
 o build connectedness across emerging and existing work in the climate and
  environmental data landscape and to help the network identify and act on 
 shared priorities\; planning and coordinating network meetings\, workshops
  and other convenings\, starting with consolidating the landscape analysis
  exercises already underway\; elevating data stewardship work by developin
 g and sharing a comprehensive picture of what is needed to sustain data cr
 itical to climate action\; supporting peer learning through surveys\, focu
 s groups or scenario planning that enable cross-organisation conversations
  about possible futures\; connecting evolving data stewardship to the broa
 der climate action and investment ecosystem\; and contributing to the Clim
 ate Reserve\, including professional development programming alongside oth
 er Climate Reserve-supported fellows. REQUIREMENTS: bachelor's degree requ
 ired\, master's or doctorate preferred\; minimum 3 years managing communit
 ies of practice (working groups\, interagency groups and similar) or coord
 inating multi-organisation or network-based initiatives\, ideally related 
 to climate or environmental science or policy\; familiarity with data stew
 ardship principles (long-term data quality\, accessibility\, interoperabil
 ity\, security) sufficient to support conversations across technical and n
 on-technical partners\; public speaking experience\; experience working ac
 ross institutional\, sectoral and geographic contexts at the science-polic
 y interface\; strong project management\, research\, analysis and synthesi
 s skills\; and willingness to travel domestically and internationally. Dom
 ain expertise in any one data stewardship area is not required\, and knowl
 edge of the civil society data stewardship space is desirable rather than 
 expected. TERM AND PAY: full-time for one year at an anticipated USD 85\,0
 00 to 100\,000. DEADLINE: priority deadline 19 August 2026. HOW TO APPLY: 
 through the AGCI Workable posting.\n\nOrganisation: Aspen Global Change In
 stitute (AGCI)\, with Public Environmental Data Partners\nLocation: Remote
